@subsection Mutable List Structure

The basic operations on pairs, the constructor and the two selectors, can be
used to construct list structure and to select parts from list structure, but
they are incapable of modifying list structure.  The same is true of the list
operations we have used so far, such as @code{append} and @code{list}, since
these can be defined in terms of the constructor and the selectors.  In this
edition the incapacity is literal: the pairs of @ref{Chapter 2} are immutable
objects.  So the section module builds its own pair: @code{mcons} makes a
record whose @code{head} field (the book's @code{car}) and @code{tail} field
(the book's @code{cdr}) assignment can change, @code{mnil} is the empty mutable
list, @code{mlist} builds a list of these pairs from its arguments, and
@code{showMList} renders one the way the book prints it, with quoted symbols
spelled as strings.  To modify list structures we need new operations.

The primitive mutators for pairs are @code{setCar} and @code{setCdr}.
@code{setCar} takes two arguments, the first of which must be a pair; its type,
@code{MCons}, says so, and the empty list is rejected before the program runs.
It modifies this pair, replacing the @code{head} pointer by a pointer to the
second argument of @code{setCar}.@footnote{The book's @code{set-car!} and
@code{set-cdr!} return implementation-dependent values.  The edition's mutators
return the modified pair, so a chain of calls reads like the book's
interactions, but like assignment they should be used only for their effect.}

@noindent
As an example, suppose that @code{x} is bound to the list @code{((a b) c d)}
and @code{y} to the list @code{(e f)} as illustrated in @ref{Figure 3.12}.
Evaluating the expression @code{setCar(x, y)} modifies the pair to which
@code{x} is bound, replacing its @code{head} by the value of @code{y}.  The
result of the operation is shown in @ref{Figure 3.13}.  The structure @code{x}
has been modified and @code{showMList(x)} would now print @code{((e f) c d)}.
The pairs representing the list @code{(a b)}, identified by the pointer that was
replaced, are now detached from the original structure.@footnote{We see from
this that mutation operations on lists can create ``garbage'' that is not part
of any accessible structure.  We will see in @ref{5.3.2} that Lisp
memory-management systems include a @newterm{garbage collector}, which
identifies and recycles the memory space used by unneeded pairs; every
JavaScript engine carries one, so a detached pair simply disappears once
nothing refers to it.}

