@noindent
@code{mcons} builds new list structure by creating new pairs, while
@code{setCar} and @code{setCdr} modify existing pairs.  Indeed, we could
implement @code{mcons} in terms of the two mutators, together with a procedure
@code{getNewPair}, which returns a new pair that is not part of any existing
list structure.  We obtain the new pair, set its @code{head} and @code{tail}
pointers to the designated objects, and return the new pair as the result of
the @code{mcons}.@footnote{@code{getNewPair} is one of the operations that
must be implemented as part of the memory management required by a Lisp
implementation.  We will discuss this in @ref{5.3.1}.  In JavaScript the object
literal plays this part, and the engine's allocator stands behind it.}

@example typescript
// The allocator's fresh pair; both fields are set before use.
const getNewPair = <A>(): MCons<A> => (@{
  _tag: "MCons", head: undefined as A, tail: mnil
@});
@end example

@example typescript
const mconsFromMutators = <A>(x: A, y: MList<A>): MCons<A> => @{
  // getNewPair is the book's allocator: a fresh pair in no list yet.
  const fresh = getNewPair<A>();
  setCar(fresh, x);
  setCdr(fresh, y);
  return fresh;
@};
@end example

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.12}Exercise 3.12:} The following procedure for
appending lists was introduced in @ref{2.2.1}, here over the section's mutable
pairs:

@example typescript
const append = <A>(x: MList<A>, y: MList<A>): MList<A> =>
  isMNil(x) ? y : mcons(x.head, append(x.tail, y));
@end example

@code{append} forms a new list by successively @code{mcons}ing the
elements of @code{x} onto @code{y}.  The procedure @code{appendBang}, the
book's @code{append!}, is similar to @code{append}, but it is a mutator rather
than a constructor.  It appends the lists by splicing them together, modifying
the final pair of @code{x} so that its @code{tail} is now @code{y}.  (It is an
error to call @code{appendBang} with an empty @code{x}; the parameter type
@code{MCons} turns that error into a type error.)

@example typescript
const appendBang = <A>(x: MCons<A>, y: MList<A>): MCons<A> => @{
  setCdr(lastPair(x), y);
  return x;
@};
@end example

Here @code{lastPair} is a procedure that returns the last pair in its
argument:

@example typescript
const lastPair = <A>(x: MCons<A>): MCons<A> =>
  isMNil(x.tail) ? x : lastPair(x.tail);
@end example

Consider the interaction

@example typescript
const x = mcons("a", mlist("b"));
const y = mlist("c", "d");
const z = append(x, y);

showMList(z)
// => "(a b c d)"

showMList(x.tail)
// ⟨@var{response}⟩

const w = appendBang(x, y);

showMList(w)
// => "(a b c d)"

showMList(x.tail)
// ⟨@var{response}⟩
@end example

What are the missing @code{⟨}@var{response}@code{⟩}s?  Draw box-and-pointer diagrams to
explain your answer.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.13}Exercise 3.13:} Consider the following
@code{makeCycle} procedure, which uses the @code{lastPair} procedure defined
in @ref{Exercise 3.12}:

@example typescript
const makeCycle = <A>(x: MCons<A>): MCons<A> => @{
  setCdr(lastPair(x), x);
  return x;
@};
@end example

Draw a box-and-pointer diagram that shows the structure @code{z} created by

@example typescript
const z = makeCycle(mcons("a", mlist("b", "c")));
@end example

What happens if we try to compute @code{lastPair(z)}?  (Do not hand @code{z}
to @code{showMList} either, for the same reason.)
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.14}Exercise 3.14:} The following procedure is quite
useful, although obscure:

@example typescript
const mystery = <A>(x: MList<A>): MList<A> => @{
  const loop = (x: MList<A>, y: MList<A>): MList<A> => @{
    if (isMNil(x)) return y;
    const temp = x.tail;
    setCdr(x, y);
    return loop(temp, x);
  @};
  return loop(x, mnil);
@};
@end example

@code{loop} uses the ``temporary'' local binding @code{temp} to hold the old
value of the @code{tail} of @code{x}, since the @code{setCdr} on the next line
destroys the @code{tail}.  Explain what @code{mystery} does in general.
Suppose @code{v} is defined by @code{const v = mlist("a", "b", "c", "d")}.
Draw the box-and-pointer diagram that represents the list to which @code{v} is
bound.  Suppose that we now evaluate @code{const w = mystery(v)}.  Draw
box-and-pointer diagrams that show the structures @code{v} and @code{w} after
evaluating this expression.  What would @code{showMList} print as the values
of @code{v} and @code{w}?
@end quotation

@subsubheading Sharing and identity

We mentioned in @ref{3.1.3} the theoretical issues of ``sameness'' and
``change'' raised by the introduction of assignment.  These issues arise in
practice when individual pairs are @newterm{shared} among different data
objects.  For example, consider the structure formed by

@example typescript
const x = mlist("a", "b");
const z1: MCons<unknown> = mcons<unknown>(x, x);
@end example

@noindent
The type argument is spelled out because the book's @code{(cons x x)} asks one
pair to hold @code{x} as its head and to hold @code{x} as a list element, and
@code{mcons} takes a head of type @code{A} with a tail of @code{MList<A>}: here
@code{A} is @code{unknown}.
As shown in @ref{Figure 3.16}, @code{z1} is a pair whose @code{head} and
@code{tail} both point to the same pair @code{x}.  This sharing of @code{x} by
the @code{head} and @code{tail} of @code{z1} is a consequence of the
straightforward way in which @code{mcons} is implemented.  In general, using
@code{mcons} to construct lists will result in an interlinked structure of
pairs in which many individual pairs are shared by many different structures.

