@noindent
In this structure, the pairs in the two @code{(a b)} lists are distinct,
although the actual symbols are shared.@footnote{The two pairs are distinct
because each call to @code{mcons} returns a new pair.  The book's symbols are
shared: in Scheme there is a unique symbol with any given name, and because
Scheme provides no way to mutate a symbol the sharing is undetectable, except
that it is what lets @code{eq?} compare symbols by checking equality of
pointers.  The edition spells those symbols as strings, which JavaScript
compares by value and does not treat as objects, so the identity tests in this
section are all tests on pairs.}

@noindent
When thought of as a list, @code{z1} and @code{z2} both represent ``the same''
list, @code{((a b) a b)}.  In general, sharing is completely undetectable if we
operate on lists using only @code{mcons} and the two selectors.  However, if we
allow mutators on list structure, sharing becomes significant.  As an example
of the difference that sharing can make, consider the following procedure,
which modifies the @code{head} of the structure to which it is applied:

@example typescript
const isPair = (v: unknown): v is MCons<unknown> =>
  typeof v === "object" && v !== null && "_tag" in v && v._tag === "MCons";

const setToWow = (x: MCons<unknown>): MCons<unknown> => @{
  const head = x.head;
  if (!isPair(head)) throw new Error("the head is not a pair");
  setCar(head, "wow");
  return x;
@};
@end example

@noindent
Even though @code{z1} and @code{z2} are ``the same'' structure, applying
@code{setToWow} to them yields different results.  With @code{z1}, altering
the @code{head} also changes the @code{tail}, because in @code{z1} the
@code{head} and the @code{tail} are the same pair.  With @code{z2}, the
@code{head} and @code{tail} are distinct, so @code{setToWow} modifies only the
@code{head}:

@example typescript
showMList(z1)
// => "((a b) a b)"

showMList(setToWow(z1))
// => "((wow b) wow b)"

showMList(z2)
// => "((a b) a b)"

showMList(setToWow(z2))
// => "((wow b) a b)"
@end example

@noindent
One way to detect sharing in list structures is to use the identity predicate
@code{Object.is}, which asks whether two values are the same object (that is,
whether they are equal as pointers).  Thus, with @code{z1} and @code{z2} as
defined in @ref{Figure 3.16} and @ref{Figure 3.17},
@code{Object.is(z1.head, z1.tail)} is true and
@code{Object.is(z2.head, z2.tail)} is false.

As will be seen in the following sections, we can exploit sharing to greatly
extend the repertoire of data structures that can be represented by pairs.  On
the other hand, sharing can also be dangerous, since modifications made to
structures will also affect other structures that happen to share the modified
parts.

The mutation operations @code{setCar} and @code{setCdr} should be
used with care; unless we have a good understanding of how our data objects are
shared, mutation can have unanticipated results.@footnote{The subtleties of
dealing with sharing of mutable data objects reflect the underlying issues of
``sameness'' and ``change'' that were raised in @ref{3.1.3}.  We
mentioned there that admitting change to our language requires that a compound
object must have an ``identity'' that is something different from the pieces
from which it is composed.  In JavaScript we consider this ``identity'' to be
the quality that is tested by @code{Object.is} applied to two objects, that is,
by equality of references.  We are ``solving the problem'' of defining the
identity of objects by stipulating that a data object ``itself'' is the
information stored in some particular object on the heap.  This suffices for
simple programs, but is hardly a general way to resolve the issue of
``sameness'' in computational models.}

