@code{z2} above.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.16}Exercise 3.16:} Ben Bitdiddle decides to write a
procedure to count the number of pairs in any list structure.  ``It's easy,''
he reasons.  ``The number of pairs in any structure is the number in the
@code{head} plus the number in the @code{tail} plus one more to count the
current pair.''  So Ben writes the following procedure:

@example typescript
const isPair = (x: unknown): x is MCons<unknown> =>
  typeof x === "object" && x !== null && "_tag" in x && x._tag === "MCons";

const countPairs = (x: unknown): number =>
  isPair(x) ? countPairs(x.head) + countPairs(x.tail) + 1 : 0;
@end example

Show that this procedure is not correct.  In particular, build, out of the
module's @code{mcons}, @code{mlist}, @code{setCar} and @code{setCdr}, list
structures holding exactly three pairs for which Ben's procedure would return
3; return 4; return 7; and one for which it never returns at all.  Draw
box-and-pointer diagrams representing the four structures, and say which of
them the section module's types can actually express.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.17}Exercise 3.17:} Devise a correct version of the
@code{countPairs} procedure, called @code{countPairsCorrect}, that returns the
number of distinct pairs in any structure.  (Hint: Traverse the structure,
maintaining an auxiliary @code{Set} that keeps track of which pairs have
already been counted.)
@end quotation

