@subsubheading Mutation is just assignment

When we introduced compound data, we observed in @ref{2.1.3} that pairs
can be represented purely in terms of procedures:

@example typescript
type PairDispatch = (m: "getX" | "getY") => number | PairDispatch;

const consPair = (x: number, y: number | PairDispatch): PairDispatch =>
  (m) => (m === "getX" ? x : y);

const getX = (z: PairDispatch): number | PairDispatch => z("getX");
const getY = (z: PairDispatch): number | PairDispatch => z("getY");
@end example

@noindent
The same observation is true for mutable data.  We can implement mutable data
objects as closures using assignment and local state.  For instance, we can
extend the above pair implementation to handle @code{setCar} and
@code{setCdr} in a manner analogous to the way we implemented bank accounts
using @code{makeAccount} in @ref{3.1.1}:

@example typescript
type PairValue = number | Pair;

interface Pair @{
  getX: () => PairValue;
  getY: () => PairValue;
  setX: (v: PairValue) => void;
  setY: (v: PairValue) => void;
@}

const makePair = (x: PairValue, y: PairValue): Pair => @{
  let head = x;
  let tail = y;
  return @{
    getX: () => head,
    getY: () => tail,
    setX: (v) => @{ head = v; @},
    setY: (v) => @{ tail = v; @},
  @};
@};
@end example

@noindent
The book's second @code{cons} answers four messages from one @code{dispatch},
and a caller reaches a setter by writing @code{((z 'set-car!) v)}.  The edition
hands the four closures back as the fields of one object, the same shape the
module uses for @code{MCons} and @code{Wire}, because a field name is the
edition's message.  The cells @code{head} and @code{tail} are still the only
place the values live, and every one of the four closures captures them.

@noindent
Assignment is all that is needed, theoretically, to account for the behavior of
mutable data.  As soon as we admit assignment to our language, we raise all
the issues, not only of assignment, but of mutable data in general.@footnote{On
the other hand, from the viewpoint of implementation, assignment requires us to
modify the environment, which is itself a mutable data structure.  Thus,
assignment and mutation are equipotent: Each can be implemented in terms of the
other.  The edition's @code{MCons} is the record form of the argument, and the
closures above are the message-passing form.}

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.20}Exercise 3.20:} @emph{SICP exercise 3.20 is
about Scheme itself, so this edition replaces it; the exercise below keeps the
number and teaches the same section material.}  The procedural pair above keeps
its two fields in the cells of one closure, so two names can alias a single
pair and a message sent through one name is visible through the other.  Draw
the closure diagram that illustrates the sequence of interactions

@example typescript
const x = makePair(1, 2);
const z = makePair(x, x);

const tail = z.getY();
if (typeof tail === "number") @{
  throw new Error("the tail of z is not a pair");
@}
tail.setX(17);

x.getX()
// => 17
@end example

@noindent
Then produce the trace @code{tracePairAliasing()} asks for: for each call in
the sequence, name the closure object that the message touches, the cell it
reads or writes, and the value left behind, and say which of @code{x},
@code{z}, and @code{z.getY()} denote the same object at the end.  (Compare
@ref{Exercise 3.11}.)
@end quotation

@node	3.3.2, 3.3.3, 3.3.1, 3.3
@subsection Representing Queues

The mutators @code{setCar} and @code{setCdr} enable us to use pairs to
construct data structures that cannot be built with the constructor and the
selectors alone.  This section shows how to use pairs to represent a data
structure called a queue.  Section @ref{3.3.3} will show how to represent data
structures called tables.

A @newterm{queue} is a sequence in which items are inserted at one end (called
the @newterm{rear} of the queue) and deleted from the other end (the
@newterm{front}).  @ref{Figure 3.18} shows an initially empty queue in which
the items @code{a} and @code{b} are inserted.  Then @code{a} is removed,
@code{c} and @code{d} are inserted, and @code{b} is removed.  Because items are
always removed in the order in which they are inserted, a queue is sometimes
called a @newterm{FIFO} (first in, first out) buffer.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.18}
@ifinfo
@quotation
@strong{Figure 3.18:} Queue operations.

@example
Operation                Resulting Queue
(define q (make-queue))
(insert-queue! q 'a)     a
(insert-queue! q 'b)     a b
(delete-queue! q)        b
(insert-queue! q 'c)     b c
(insert-queue! q 'd)     b c d
(delete-queue! q)        c d
@end example
@end quotation
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.18,,119mm,Figure 3.18: Queue operations.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.18.std,,119mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.18:} Queue operations.}
@end float

@noindent
In terms of data abstraction, we can regard a queue as defined by the following
set of operations:

@itemize @bullet

@item
a constructor: @code{makeQueue()} returns an empty queue (a queue containing
no items).

@item
two selectors:

@example typescript
isEmptyQueue(⟨@var{queue}⟩)
@end example

@noindent
tests if the queue is empty.

@example typescript
frontQueue(⟨@var{queue}⟩)
@end example

@noindent
returns the object at the front of the queue, throwing
@code{EmptyQueueError} if the queue is empty; it does not modify the queue.

@item
two mutators:

@example typescript
insertQueue(⟨@var{queue}⟩, ⟨@var{item}⟩)
@end example

@noindent
inserts the item at the rear of the queue and returns the modified queue as its
value.

@example typescript
deleteQueue(⟨@var{queue}⟩)
@end example

@noindent
removes the item at the front of the queue and returns the modified queue as
its value, throwing @code{EmptyQueueError} if the queue is empty before the
deletion.

@end itemize

@noindent
Because a queue is a sequence of items, we could certainly represent it as an
ordinary list; the front of the queue would be the @code{head} of the list,
inserting an item in the queue would amount to appending a new element at the
end of the list, and deleting an item from the queue would just be taking the
@code{tail} of the list.  However, this representation is inefficient, because
in order to insert an item we must scan the list until we reach the end.  Since
the only method we have for scanning a list is by successive @code{tail}
operations, this scanning requires @math{{\Theta(n)}} steps for a list of @math{n}
items.  A simple modification to the list representation overcomes this
disadvantage by allowing the queue operations to be implemented so that they
require @math{{\Theta(1)}} steps; that is, so that the number of steps needed is
independent of the length of the queue.

The difficulty with the list representation arises from the need to scan to
find the end of the list.  The reason we need to scan is that, although the
standard way of representing a list as a chain of pairs readily provides us
with a pointer to the beginning of the list, it gives us no easily accessible
pointer to the end.  The modification that avoids the drawback is to represent
the queue as a list, together with an additional pointer that indicates the
final pair in the list.  That way, when we go to insert an item, we can consult
the rear pointer and so avoid scanning the list.

@noindent
A queue is represented, then, as a pair of pointers, @code{front} and
@code{rear}, which indicate, respectively, the first and last pairs in an
ordinary list.  Since we would like the queue to be an identifiable object, the
edition keeps the two pointers in the two mutable fields of one object, which
plays the part of the book's @code{cons} of the two pointers.  @ref{Figure
3.19} illustrates this representation.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.19}
@ifinfo
@quotation
@strong{Figure 3.19:} Implementation of a queue as a list with front and rear pointers.

@example
       +---+---+
  q -->| * | *-+-------------------+
       +-|-+---+                   |
         |                         |
         | front-ptr               | rear-ptr
         V                         V
     +---+---+    +---+---+    +---+---+
     | * | *-+--->| * | *-+--->| * | / |
     +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+
       V            V            V
     +---+        +---+        +---+
     | a |        | b |        | c |
     +---+        +---+        +---+
@end example
@end quotation
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.19b,,118mm,Figure 3.19: Implementation of a queue as a list with front and rear pointers.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.19b.std,,118mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.19:} Implementation of a queue as a list with front and rear pointers.}
@end float

To define the queue operations we use the following procedures, which enable us
to select and to modify the front and rear pointers of a queue:

@example typescript
const frontPtr = <A>(queue: Queue<A>): MList<A> => queue.front;
const rearPtr = <A>(queue: Queue<A>): MCons<A> | null => queue.rear;
const setFrontPtr = <A>(queue: Queue<A>, item: MList<A>): Queue<A> => @{
  queue.front = item;
  return queue;
@};
const setRearPtr = <A>(queue: Queue<A>, item: MCons<A> | null): Queue<A> => @{
  queue.rear = item;
  return queue;
@};
@end example

@noindent
Now we can implement the actual queue operations.  We will consider a queue to
be empty if its front pointer is the empty list:

@example typescript
const isEmptyQueue = <A>(queue: Queue<A>): boolean => isMNil(queue.front);
@end example

@noindent
The @code{makeQueue} constructor returns, as an initially empty queue, an
object whose @code{front} pointer is empty and whose @code{rear} pointer is
null, the edition's spelling of a pointer to nothing:

@example typescript
const makeQueue = <A>(): Queue<A> => (@{ front: mnil, rear: null @});
@end example

@noindent
To select the item at the front of the queue, we return the @code{head} of the
pair indicated by the front pointer:

@example typescript
const frontQueue = <A>(queue: Queue<A>): A => @{
  const front = queue.front;
  if (isMNil(front)) @{
    throw new EmptyQueueError("FRONT");
  @}
  return front.head;
@};
@end example

@noindent
To insert an item in a queue, we follow the method whose result is indicated in
@ref{Figure 3.20}.  We first create a new pair whose @code{head} is the item to
be inserted and whose @code{tail} is the empty list.  If the queue was initially
empty, we set the front and rear pointers of the queue to this new pair.
Otherwise, we modify the final pair in the queue to point to the new pair, and
also set the rear pointer to the new pair.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.20}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.20:} Result of using @code{(insert-queue! q 'd)} on the queue of @ref{Figure 3.19}.

@example
       +---+---+
  q -->| * | *-+--------------------------------+
       +-|-+---+                                |
         |                                      |
         | front-ptr                            | rear-ptr
         V                                      V
     +---+---+    +---+---+    +---+---+    +---+---+
     | * | *-+--->| * | *-+--->| * | *-+--->| * | / |
     +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+
       V            V            V            V
     +---+        +---+        +---+        +---+
     | a |        | b |        | c |        | d |
     +---+        +---+        +---+        +---+
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.20c,,141mm,Figure 3.20: Result of using (insert-queue! q 'd) on the queue of Figure 3.19.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.20c.std,,141mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.20:} Result of using @code{(insert-queue! q 'd)} on the queue of @ref{Figure 3.19}.}
@end float

@example typescript
const insertQueue = <A>(queue: Queue<A>, item: A): Queue<A> => @{
  const newPair = mcons(item, mnil);
  if (isEmptyQueue(queue)) @{
    setFrontPtr(queue, newPair);
    setRearPtr(queue, newPair);
    return queue;
  @}
  const rear = rearPtr(queue);
  if (rear === null) @{
    throw new Error("corrupt queue: nonempty front with null rear");
  @}
  setCdr(rear, newPair);
  setRearPtr(queue, newPair);
  return queue;
@};
@end example

@noindent
To delete the item at the front of the queue, we merely modify the front
pointer so that it now points at the second item in the queue, which can be
found by following the @code{tail} pointer of the first item (see
@ref{Figure 3.21}):@footnote{If the first item is the final item in the queue,
the front pointer will be the empty list after the deletion, which will mark
the queue as empty; the book needn't worry about updating the rear pointer,
which will still point to the deleted item, because @code{isEmptyQueue} looks
only at the front pointer.  The edition clears the rear pointer in this case
anyway, because a null rear is cheaper to reason about than a pointer into a
detached pair.}

@example typescript
const deleteQueue = <A>(queue: Queue<A>): Queue<A> => @{
  const front = queue.front;
  if (isMNil(front)) @{
    throw new EmptyQueueError("DELETE!");
  @}
  setFrontPtr(queue, front.tail);
  if (isMNil(front.tail)) @{
    setRearPtr(queue, null);
  @}
  return queue;
@};
@end example

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.21}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.21:} Result of using @code{(delete-queue!  q)} on the queue of @ref{Figure 3.20}.

@example
       +---+---+
  q -->| * | *-+--------------------------------+
       +-|-+---+                                |
         +------------+                         |
            front-ptr |                         | rear-ptr
                      V                         V
     +---+---+    +---+---+    +---+---+    +---+---+
     | * | *-+--->| * | *-+--->| * | *-+--->| * | / |
     +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+
       V            V            V            V
     +---+        +---+        +---+        +---+
     | a |        | b |        | c |        | d |
     +---+        +---+        +---+        +---+
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.21c,,141mm,Figure 3.21: Result of using (delete-queue! q) on the queue of Figure 3.20.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.21c.std,,141mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.21:} Result of using @code{(delete-queue!  q)} on the queue of @ref{Figure 3.20}.}
@end float

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.21}Exercise 3.21:} Ben Bitdiddle decides to test the
queue implementation described above.  He types in the procedures and proceeds
to try them out:

@example typescript
const q1 = makeQueue<string>();

insertQueue(q1, "a");
// q1.front: (a)   q1.rear: (a)

insertQueue(q1, "b");
// q1.front: (a b)   q1.rear: (b)

deleteQueue(q1);
// q1.front: (b)   q1.rear: (b)

deleteQueue(q1);
// q1.front: ()   q1.rear: null
@end example

@noindent
``It's all wrong!'' he complains.  ``The printed object shows the last
item twice, once in @code{front} and once in @code{rear}, so it looks as if
every insertion went in twice.''  Eva Lu Ator suggests that Ben has
misunderstood what is happening.  ``It's not that the items are going into
the queue twice,'' she explains.  ``It's just that the default printer
doesn't know how to make sense of the queue representation.  It prints the
object's two pointer fields one after the other: @code{front}, the chain of
all the items, and @code{rear}, the one-item list that starts at the last
pair, so the newest item shows up in both renderings.  If you want to see
the queue printed correctly, you'll have to define your own print procedure
for queues.''  Explain what Eva Lu is talking about.  In particular, show
why Ben's examples produce the printed results that they do.  Define a
procedure @code{printQueue} that takes a queue as input and returns the
sequence of items in the queue as a string, and Ben's
@code{benPrintQueue}, which renders the raw object and so exposes the rear
pointer.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.22}Exercise 3.22:} Instead of representing a queue
as an object with two pointer fields, we can build a queue as a closure with
local state.  The local state will consist of pointers to the beginning and the
end of an ordinary list.  Thus, the @code{makeQueueClosure} procedure will have
the form

@example typescript
type QueueDispatch = (m: string) => unknown;
@end example

@example typescript
const makeQueueClosure = (): QueueDispatch => @{
  let frontPtr: MList<string> = mnil;
  let rearPtr: MCons<string> | null = null;
  // ⟨@var{definitions of internal procedures}⟩
  const dispatch = (m: string): unknown => @{ ... @};
  return dispatch;
@};
@end example

@noindent
Complete the definition of @code{makeQueueClosure}, whose dispatch accepts the
string messages @code{"insert!"}, @code{"delete!"}, @code{"front"},
@code{"empty?"} and @code{"print"}, and provide implementations of the queue
operations using this representation.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.23}Exercise 3.23:} A @newterm{deque} (``double-ended
queue'') is a sequence in which items can be inserted and deleted at either the
front or the rear.  Operations on deques are the constructor @code{makeDeque},
the predicate @code{isEmptyDeque}, selectors @code{frontDeque} and
@code{rearDeque}, and mutators @code{insertFrontDeque},
@code{insertRearDeque}, @code{deleteFrontDeque}, @code{deleteRearDeque}.  Show
how to represent deques using the section module's mutable pairs, with a
doubly linked node @code{DequeNode} carrying @code{item}, @code{prev} and
@code{next} fields, and give implementations of the
operations.@footnote{Be careful not to make the printer try to render a
structure that contains cycles.  (See @ref{Exercise 3.13}.)}
All operations should be accomplished in @math{{\Theta(1)}} steps.
@end quotation

@node	3.3.3, 3.3.4, 3.3.2, 3.3
@subsection Representing Tables

When we studied various ways of representing sets in @ref{Chapter 2}, we
mentioned in @ref{2.3.3} the task of maintaining a table of records
indexed by identifying keys.  In the implementation of data-directed
programming in @ref{2.4.3}, we made extensive use of two-dimensional
tables, in which information is stored and retrieved using two keys.  Here we
see how to build tables as mutable list structures.

We first consider a one-dimensional table, in which each value is stored under
a single key.  We implement the table as a list of records, each of which is
implemented as a pair consisting of a key and the associated value; the
edition's @code{TableRecord} is exactly that pair, with @code{key} and
@code{value} fields.  The records
are glued together to form a list by pairs whose @code{head}s point to
successive records.  These gluing pairs are called the @newterm{backbone} of
the table.  In order to have a place that we can change when we add a new
record to the table, the book builds the table as a @newterm{headed list}.  A
headed list has a special backbone pair at the beginning, which holds a dummy
``record''---in this case the arbitrarily chosen symbol @code{*table*}.  The
edition keeps the same role in a smaller costume: the @code{Table} object's one
mutable @code{records} field is the head that the book's dummy pair holds, and
the record chain hangs off it.

@ref{Figure 3.22} shows the box-and-pointer diagram for the table

@example
a:  1
b:  2
c:  3
@end example

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.22}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.22:} A table represented as a headed list.

@example
+---+---+    +---+---+    +---+---+    +---+---+
| * | *-+--->| * | *-+--->| * | *-+--->| * | / |
+-|-+---+    +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+    +-|-+---+
|            |            |            |
V            V            V            V
+---------+   +---+---+   +---+---+   +---+---+
| *table* |   | * | * |   | * | * |   | * | * |
+---------+   +-|-+-|-+   +-|-+-|-+   +-|-+-|-+
|   |       |   |       |   |
V   V       V   V       V   V
+---+ +---+ +---+ +---+ +---+ +---+
| a | | 1 | | b | | 2 | | c | | 3 |
+---+ +---+ +---+ +---+ +---+ +---+
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.22c,,138mm,Figure 3.22: A table represented as a headed list.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.22c.std,,138mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.22:} A table represented as a headed list.}
@end float

@noindent
To extract information from a table we use the @code{tableLookup} procedure,
which takes a key as argument and returns the associated value (or
@code{undefined} if there is no value stored under that key, the edition's
spelling of the book's false).  @code{tableLookup} scans the record chain
directly: it is defined in terms of the @code{assoc} operation, which
expects a key and a list of records as arguments.  Note that @code{assoc} never
sees the dummy record.  @code{Assoc} returns the record that has the given key
as its @code{key}.@footnote{The book's @code{assoc} compares keys with
@code{equal?}, so it can recognize keys that are symbols, numbers, or list
structure.  The edition's default @code{sameKey} test is strict equality, which
covers the strings and numbers this section's keys are; @ref{Exercise 3.24}
opens the test to whatever comparison a caller supplies.}  @code{tableLookup}
then checks to see that the record it found is not absent, and returns the
value (the @code{value} field) of the record.

@example typescript
const tableLookup = <K, V>(
  table: Table<K, V>,
  key: K,
  sameKey: (a: K, b: K) => boolean = sameKeyDefault,
): V | undefined => @{
  const record = assoc(key, table.records, sameKey);
  return record === undefined ? undefined : record.value;
@};

const assoc = <K, V>(
  key: K,
  records: MList<TableRecord<K, V>>,
  sameKey: (a: K, b: K) => boolean = sameKeyDefault,
): TableRecord<K, V> | undefined => @{
  for (let rest = records; !isMNil(rest); rest = rest.tail) @{
    if (sameKey(rest.head.key, key)) return rest.head;
  @}
  return undefined;
@};
@end example

@noindent
To insert a value in a table under a specified key, we first use @code{assoc}
to see if there is already a record in the table with this key.  If not, we
form a new record out of the key and the value, and insert this at
the head of the table's list of records, after the dummy record.  If there
already is a record with this key, we set the @code{value} of this record to
the designated new value.  The header of the table provides us with a fixed
location to modify in order to insert the new record.@footnote{Thus, the first
backbone pair is the object that represents the table ``itself''; that is, a
pointer to the table is a pointer to this pair.  This same backbone pair always
starts the table.  If we did not arrange things in this way, @code{tableInsert}
would have to return a new value for the start of the table when it added a new
record.  In the edition the @code{records} field of the @code{Table} object
plays the part of that first backbone pair.}

@example typescript
const tableInsert = <K, V>(
  table: Table<K, V>,
  key: K,
  value: V,
  sameKey: (a: K, b: K) => boolean = sameKeyDefault,
): Table<K, V> => @{
  const record = assoc(key, table.records, sameKey);
  if (record !== undefined) @{
    record.value = value;
  @} else @{
    table.records = mcons(@{ key, value @}, table.records);
  @}
  return table;
@};
@end example

@noindent
To construct a new table, we simply create an object with an empty record
chain, which is the edition's empty headed list:

@example typescript
const makeTable = <K, V>(): Table<K, V> => (@{ records: mnil @});
@end example

@subsubheading Two-dimensional tables

In a two-dimensional table, each value is indexed by two keys.  We can
construct such a table as a one-dimensional table in which each key identifies
a subtable.  @ref{Figure 3.23} shows the box-and-pointer diagram for the table

@example
math:  +: 43    letters:  a: 97
       -: 45              b: 98
       *: 42
@end example

@noindent
which has two subtables.  (The subtables don't need a special header symbol,
since the key that identifies the subtable serves this purpose.)

