@noindent
When we look up an item, we use the first key to identify the correct subtable.
Then we use the second key to identify the record within the subtable.

@example typescript
const tableLookup2 = <K1, K2, V>(
  table: Table2<K1, K2, V>,
  key1: K1,
  key2: K2,
): V | undefined => @{
  const subtable = assoc(key1, table.records);
  if (subtable === undefined) return undefined;
  return tableLookup(subtable.value, key2);
@};
@end example

@noindent
To insert a new item under a pair of keys, we use @code{assoc} to see if there
is a subtable stored under the first key.  If not, we build a new subtable
containing the single record (@code{key-2}, @code{value}) and insert it into
the table under the first key.  If a subtable already exists for the first key,
we insert the new record into this subtable, using the insertion method for
one-dimensional tables described above:

@example typescript
const tableInsert2 = <K1, K2, V>(
  table: Table2<K1, K2, V>,
  key1: K1,
  key2: K2,
  value: V,
): void => @{
  const subtable = assoc(key1, table.records);
  if (subtable !== undefined) @{
    tableInsert(subtable.value, key2, value);
    return;
  @}
  const fresh = makeTable<K2, V>();
  tableInsert(fresh, key2, value);
  table.records = mcons(@{ key: key1, value: fresh @}, table.records);
@};
@end example

@subsubheading Creating local tables

The @code{tableLookup} and @code{tableInsert} operations defined above take the
table as an argument.  This enables us to use programs that access more than one
table.
Another way to deal with multiple tables is to have separate @code{lookup} and
@code{insert} procedures for each table.  We can do this by representing a
table procedurally, as an object that maintains an internal table as part of
its local state.  When sent an appropriate message, this ``table object''
supplies the procedure with which to operate on the internal table.  Here is a
generator for two-dimensional tables represented in this fashion:

@example typescript
interface LocalTable<K1, K2, V> @{
  lookup: (key1: K1, key2: K2) => V | undefined;
  insert: (key1: K1, key2: K2, value: V) => void;
@}

const makeLocalTable = <K1, K2, V>(): LocalTable<K1, K2, V> => @{
  const localTable = makeTable2<K1, K2, V>();
  return @{
    lookup: (key1, key2) => tableLookup2(localTable, key1, key2),
    insert: (key1, key2, value) => @{
      tableInsert2(localTable, key1, key2, value);
    @},
  @};
@};
@end example

@noindent
The book's generator answers the messages @code{lookup-proc} and
@code{insert-proc!} from one @code{dispatch}; the edition names the two
closures as fields of the object it returns, and the internal table is
reachable through no other door.

@noindent
Using @code{makeLocalTable}, we could implement the @code{get} and @code{put}
operations used in @ref{2.4.3} for data-directed programming, as
follows:

@example typescript
const operationTable = makeLocalTable<string, string, () => unknown>();
const get = operationTable.lookup;
const put = operationTable.insert;
@end example

@noindent
@code{get} takes as arguments two keys, and @code{put} takes as arguments two
keys and a value.  Both operations access the same local table, which is
encapsulated within the object created by the call to @code{makeLocalTable}.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.24}Exercise 3.24:} In the table implementations
above, the keys are compared with the default @code{sameKey} test, strict
equality, where the book uses @code{equal?} inside @code{assoc}.  This is not
always the appropriate test.  For instance, we
might have a table with numeric keys in which we don't need an exact match to
the number we're looking up, but only a number within some tolerance of it.
Design a table constructor @code{makeTableSameKey} that takes as an argument a
@code{sameKey} procedure that will be used to test ``equality'' of keys.
@code{makeTableSameKey} should return an object with @code{lookup} and
@code{insert} methods for a local table.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.25}Exercise 3.25:} Generalizing one- and
two-dimensional tables, show how to implement a table in which values are
stored under an arbitrary number of keys and different values may be stored
under different numbers of keys.  @code{makeNestedTable} should return an
object whose @code{lookup} and @code{insert} methods take as input an array of
keys used to access the table.
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.26}Exercise 3.26:} To search a table as implemented
above, one needs to scan through the list of records.  This is basically the
unordered list representation of @ref{2.3.3}.  For large tables, it may
be more efficient to structure the table in a different manner.  Describe a
@code{makeTreeTable(compare)} implementation where the (key, value) records are
organized using a binary tree, assuming that keys can be ordered in some way
(e.g., numerically or alphabetically), and give @code{keysInOrder} as well.
(Compare @ref{Exercise 2.66} of @ref{Chapter 2}.)
@end quotation

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.27}Exercise 3.27:} @newterm{Memoization} (also
called @newterm{tabulation}) is a technique that enables a procedure to record,
in a local table, values that have previously been computed.  This technique
can make a vast difference in the performance of a program.  A memoized
procedure maintains a table in which values of previous calls are stored using
as keys the arguments that produced the values.  When the memoized procedure is
asked to compute a value, it first checks the table to see if the value is
already there and, if so, just returns that value.  Otherwise, it computes the
new value in the ordinary way and stores this in the table.  As an example of
memoization, recall from @ref{1.2.2} the exponential process for
computing Fibonacci numbers:

@example typescript
const fib = (n: number): number =>
  n === 0 ? 0 : n === 1 ? 1 : fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2);
@end example

The memoized version of the same procedure is

@example typescript
const memoFib: (n: number) => number = memoize(
  (n: number): number =>
    n === 0 ? 0 : n === 1 ? 1 : memoFib(n - 1) + memoFib(n - 2),
);
@end example

@noindent
where the memoizer keeps a @code{Map} as its table, the edition's mutable
lookup structure for keys that hash:

@example typescript
const memoize = <A, B>(f: (x: A) => B): ((x: A) => B) => @{
  const table = new Map<A, B>();
  return (x: A): B => @{
    const previouslyComputedResult = table.get(x);
    if (previouslyComputedResult !== undefined) return previouslyComputedResult;
    const result = f(x);
    table.set(x, result);
    return result;
  @};
@};
@end example

@noindent
Draw the closure structure that analyzes the computation of
@code{memoFib(3)}, or produce the trace @code{memoFibTrace(3)} asks for: the
sequence of @code{Compute} and @code{Recall} events, in order, with the key
each one touches.  Explain why @code{memoFib} computes the
@math{n^{\hbox{th}}} Fibonacci number in a number
of steps proportional to @math{n}.  Would the scheme still work if we had simply
defined @code{memoFib} to be @code{memoize(fib)}?
@end quotation

@node	3.3.4, 3.3.5, 3.3.3, 3.3
@subsection A Simulator for Digital Circuits

Designing complex digital systems, such as computers, is an important
engineering activity.  Digital systems are constructed by interconnecting
simple elements.  Although the behavior of these individual elements is simple,
networks of them can have very complex behavior.  Computer simulation of
proposed circuit designs is an important tool used by digital systems
engineers.  In this section we design a system for performing digital logic
simulations.  This system typifies a kind of program called an
@newterm{event-driven simulation}, in which actions (``events'') trigger
further events that happen at a later time, which in turn trigger more events,
and so on.

Our computational model of a circuit will be composed of objects that
correspond to the elementary components from which the circuit is constructed.
There are @newterm{wires}, which carry @newterm{digital signals}.  A digital
signal may at any moment have only one of two possible values, which the
edition spells @code{false} and @code{true} where the book writes 0 and 1.
There are also various types of digital @newterm{function boxes}, which connect
wires carrying input signals to other output wires.  Such boxes produce output
signals computed from their input signals.  The output signal is delayed by a
time that depends on the type of the function box.  For example, an
@newterm{inverter} is a primitive function box that inverts its input.  If the
input signal to an inverter changes to @code{false}, then one inverter-delay
later the inverter will change its output signal to @code{true}.  If the input
signal to an inverter changes to @code{true}, then one inverter-delay later the
inverter will change its output signal to @code{false}.  We draw an inverter
symbolically as in @ref{Figure 3.24}.
An @newterm{and-gate}, also shown in figure 3.24, is a primitive
function box with two inputs and one output.  It drives its output signal to a
value that is the @newterm{logical and} of the inputs.  That is, if both of its
input signals become @code{true}, then one and-gate-delay time later the
and-gate will force its output signal to be @code{true}; otherwise the output
will be @code{false}.  An
@newterm{or-gate} is a similar two-input primitive function box that drives its
output signal to a value that is the @newterm{logical or} of the inputs.  That
is, the output will become @code{true} if at least one of the input signals is
@code{true}; otherwise the output will become @code{false}.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.24}
@ifinfo
@quotation
