@noindent
Having defined @code{fullAdder} as a procedure, we can now use it as a
building block for creating still more complex circuits.  (For example, see
@ref{Exercise 3.30}.)

In essence, our simulator provides us with the tools to construct a language of
circuits.  If we adopt the general perspective on languages with which we
approached the study of Lisp in @ref{1.1}, we can say that the
primitive function boxes form the primitive elements of the language, that
wiring boxes together provides a means of combination, and that specifying
wiring patterns as procedures serves as a means of abstraction.

@subsubheading Primitive function boxes

The primitive function boxes implement the ``forces'' by which a change in the
signal on one wire influences the signals on other wires.  To build function
boxes, we use the following operations on wires:

@itemize @bullet

@item @code{wire.getSignal()}

@noindent
returns the current value of the signal on the wire.

@item @code{wire.setSignal(⟨@var{new value}⟩)}

@noindent
changes the value of the signal on the wire to the new value.

@item @code{wire.addAction(⟨@var{procedure of no arguments}⟩)}

@noindent
asserts that the designated procedure should be run whenever the signal on the
wire changes value.  Such procedures are the vehicles by which changes in the
signal value on the wire are communicated to other wires.

@end itemize

@noindent
In addition, we will make use of a procedure @code{afterDelay} that takes a
time delay, a procedure to be run, and the agenda to put it on, and schedules
the given procedure after the given delay.

Using these procedures, we can define the primitive digital logic functions.
To connect an input to an output through an inverter, we use @code{addAction}
to associate with the input wire a procedure that will be run whenever the
signal on the input wire changes value.  The procedure computes the
@code{logicalNot} of the input signal, and then, after one
@code{inverterDelay}, sets the output signal to be this new value:

@example typescript
const inverter = (input: Wire, output: Wire, agenda: Agenda): void => @{
  const invertInput = (): void => @{
    afterDelay(inverterDelay, () => output.setSignal(logicalNot(input.getSignal())), agenda);
  @};
  input.addAction(invertInput);
@};

const logicalNot = (value: boolean): boolean => !value;
@end example

@noindent
The book's @code{logical-not} also signals an error for a signal that is
neither 0 nor 1; here the parameter type @code{boolean} rules that out before
the program runs.

@noindent
An and-gate is a little more complex.  The action procedure must be run if
either of the inputs to the gate changes.  It schedules an action that, after
one @code{andGateDelay}, computes the @code{logicalAnd} (using a procedure
analogous to @code{logicalNot}) of the values of the signals on the input wires
and writes the result to the output.  The book computes that value when the
action procedure runs and schedules only the write; the edition reads the
inputs when the delayed action fires, which is the difference the sample
simulation below shows.

@example typescript
const andGate = (a1: Wire, a2: Wire, output: Wire, agenda: Agenda): void => @{
  const andActionProcedure = (): void => @{
    afterDelay(
      andGateDelay,
      () => output.setSignal(logicalAnd(a1.getSignal(), a2.getSignal())),
      agenda,
    );
  @};
  a1.addAction(andActionProcedure);
  a2.addAction(andActionProcedure);
@};
@end example

