@subsubheading Representing wires

A wire in our simulation will be a computational object with two pieces of
local state: a signal value (initially taken to be @code{false}) and a
collection of action procedures to be run when the signal changes value.  We
implement the wire, using message-passing style, as a closure over those two
variables that hands back the operations an outside caller may ask for, just as
we did with the simple bank-account object in @ref{3.1.1}:

@example typescript
const makeWire = (): Wire => @{
  let signal = false;
  let actions: Array<() => void> = [];
  const setMySignal = (newValue: boolean): void => @{
    if (signal !== newValue) @{
      signal = newValue;
      callEach(actions);
    @}
  @};
  const acceptActionProcedure = (proc: () => void): void => @{
    actions = [proc, ...actions];
    proc();
  @};
  return @{ getSignal: () => signal, setSignal: setMySignal, addAction: acceptActionProcedure @};
@};
@end example

@noindent
The module spells the three local operations as the fields of a @code{Wire}
interface, which is the edition's object in place of the book's @code{dispatch}
procedure.

@noindent
The local procedure @code{setMySignal} tests whether the new signal value
changes the signal on the wire.  If so, it runs each of the action procedures,
using the following procedure @code{callEach}, which calls each of the items
in a list of no-argument procedures:

@example typescript
const callEach = (procedures: Array<() => void>): void => @{
  for (const procedure of procedures) @{
    procedure();
  @}
@};
@end example

@noindent
The local procedure @code{acceptActionProcedure} adds the given procedure to
the list of procedures to be run, and then runs the new procedure once.  (See
@ref{Exercise 3.31}.)

With those local operations set up as specified, the object the constructor
returns is the only way to reach them, and a caller writes
@code{wire.getSignal()}, @code{wire.setSignal(v)}, or
@code{wire.addAction(p)}:@footnote{@anchor{Footnote 155} These method names are
simply syntactic sugar that allow us to use ordinary procedural syntax to
access the local procedures of objects.  It is striking that we can
interchange the role of ``procedures'' and ``data'' in such a simple way.  For
example, writing @code{wire.getSignal()} we think of @code{wire} as an object
that is asked for its signal.  Alternatively, a free procedure written
@code{getSignal(wire)} encourages us to think of @code{wire} as a data object
that is the input to a procedure @code{getSignal}.  The truth of the matter is
that, in a language in which we can deal with procedures as objects, there is
no fundamental difference between ``procedures'' and ``data,'' and we can
choose our syntactic sugar to allow us to program in whatever style we choose.}

@example typescript
const getSignal = (wire: Wire): boolean => wire.getSignal();
const setSignal = (wire: Wire, newValue: boolean): void =>
  wire.setSignal(newValue);
const addAction = (wire: Wire, actionProcedure: () => void): void =>
  wire.addAction(actionProcedure);
@end example

@noindent
Wires, which have time-varying signals and may be incrementally attached to
devices, are typical of mutable objects.  We have modeled them as closures
over local state variables that are modified by assignment.  When a new wire is
created, a new set of state variables is allocated (by the @code{let}
declarations in @code{makeWire}) and a new result object is
constructed and returned, its methods capturing the state
variables in their closures.

The wires are shared among the various devices that have been connected to
them.  Thus, a change made by an interaction with one device will affect all
the other devices attached to the wire.  The wire communicates the change to
its neighbors by calling the action procedures provided to it when the
connections were established.

@subsubheading The agenda

The only thing needed to complete the simulator is @code{afterDelay}.  The
idea here is that we maintain a data structure, called an @newterm{agenda},
that contains a schedule of things to do.  The following operations are defined
for agendas:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{makeAgenda()} returns a new empty agenda.

@item
@code{isAgendaEmpty(⟨@var{agenda}⟩)} is true if the specified agenda is
empty.

@item
@code{firstAgendaItem(⟨@var{agenda}⟩)} returns the first item on the
agenda.

@item
@code{removeFirstAgendaItem(⟨@var{agenda}⟩)} modifies the agenda by
removing the first item.

@item
@code{addToAgenda(⟨@var{time}⟩, ⟨@var{action}⟩, ⟨@var{agenda}⟩)}
modifies the agenda by adding the given action procedure to be run at the
specified time.

@item
@code{currentAgendaTime(⟨@var{agenda}⟩)} returns the current simulation time.

@end itemize

@noindent
The book keeps one particular agenda in a global named @code{the-agenda}.  The
edition has no such global: every procedure that schedules or runs work takes
the agenda it works on as a parameter, so two simulations can run side by side
and a test can start from a fresh agenda.  The procedure @code{afterDelay} adds
new elements to the agenda it is given:

@example typescript
const afterDelay = (delay: number, action: () => void, agenda: Agenda): void =>
  addToAgenda(currentAgendaTime(agenda) + delay, action, agenda);
@end example

@noindent
The simulation is driven by the procedure @code{propagate}, which operates on
the agenda it is handed, executing each procedure on that agenda in sequence.
In
general, as the simulation runs, new items will be added to the agenda, and
@code{propagate} will continue the simulation as long as there are items on the
agenda:

@example typescript
const propagate = (agenda: Agenda): void => @{
  while (!isAgendaEmpty(agenda)) @{
    firstAgendaItem(agenda)();
    removeFirstAgendaItem(agenda);
  @}
@};
@end example

@subsubheading A sample simulation

The following procedure, which places a ``probe'' on a wire, shows the
simulator in action.  The probe tells the wire that, whenever its signal
changes value, it should record the new signal value, together with the current
time and a name that identifies the wire.  The book prints those lines; the
edition appends one @code{ProbeEvent} to an events array the caller owns, so a
test can read what the simulation said:

@example typescript
const probe = (
  name: string,
  wire: Wire,
  agenda: Agenda,
  events: ProbeEvent[],
): void => @{
  wire.addAction(() => @{
    events.push(@{ time: currentAgendaTime(agenda), name, value: wire.getSignal() @});
  @});
@};
@end example

@noindent
We begin by building an agenda; the delays of the primitive
function boxes are the module's constants @code{inverterDelay} = 2,
@code{andGateDelay} = 3, and @code{orGateDelay} = 5, in the book's time units:

@example typescript
const agenda = makeAgenda();
const events: ProbeEvent[] = [];
@end example

@noindent
Now we define four wires, placing probes on two of them:

@example typescript
const input1 = makeWire();
const input2 = makeWire();
const sum = makeWire();
const carry = makeWire();

probe("sum", sum, agenda, events)
// events ends with @i{@{ time: 0, name: "sum", value: false @}}

probe("carry", carry, agenda, events)
// events ends with @i{@{ time: 0, name: "carry", value: false @}}
@end example

@noindent
Next we connect the wires in a half-adder circuit (as in @ref{Figure 3.25}),
set the signal on @code{input1} to @code{true}, and run the simulation:

@example typescript
halfAdder(input1, input2, sum, carry, agenda);

input1.setSignal(true);

propagate(agenda)
// events gains @i{@{ time: 5, name: "sum", value: true @}}
@end example

@noindent
The @code{sum} signal changes to @code{true} at time 5, and the agenda's clock
has run on to time 8: we are now eight time units from the beginning of the
simulation.  This edition and the book part company at time 5, because the
book's run prints @i{sum 8 New-value = 1} here: the book's action
procedure computes the new value when it runs and schedules only the write,
while the gates above read their inputs inside the delayed action, at fire
time, so the and-gate response that the attach-time initialization schedules
for time 5 finds the or-gate's wire already raised by the item ahead of it in
the same segment.  This edition pins what its own simulator prints, so the
transcript above is the run's output and not the book's.

At this point, we can set the signal on @code{input2} to @code{true} and allow
the values to propagate:

@example typescript
input2.setSignal(true);

propagate(agenda)
// events gains @i{@{ time: 11, name: "carry", value: true @}}
// events gains @i{@{ time: 16, name: "sum", value: false @}}
@end example

@noindent
The @code{carry} changes to @code{true} at time 11 and the @code{sum} changes
to @code{false} at time 16, exactly the two readings the book reports.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.31}Exercise 3.31:} The internal procedure
@code{acceptActionProcedure} defined in @code{makeWire} specifies that when
a new action procedure is added to a wire, the procedure is immediately run.
Explain why this initialization is necessary.  In particular, trace through the
half-adder example in the paragraphs above and say how the system's response
would differ if we had defined @code{acceptActionProcedure} as

@example typescript
const acceptActionProcedure = (proc: () => void): void => @{
  actions = [proc, ...actions];
@};
@end example

@noindent
Build that variant as @code{makeWireWithoutInitRun}, attach the half-adder and
the probes as the sample simulation does, and report what
@code{demonstrateWithoutInitRun()} finds in the events array.
@end quotation

@subsubheading Implementing the agenda

Finally, we give details of the agenda data structure, which holds the
procedures that are scheduled for future execution.

The agenda is made up of @newterm{time segments}.  Each time segment is a pair
consisting of a number (the time) and a queue (see @ref{Exercise 3.32}) that
holds the procedures that are scheduled to be run during that time segment.

@example typescript
interface TimeSegment @{
  readonly time: number;
  readonly queue: Queue<() => void>;
@}
@end example

@noindent
We will operate on the time-segment queues using the queue operations described
in @ref{3.3.2}.

The agenda itself is a collection of time segments.  It differs from
the tables described in @ref{3.3.3} in that the segments will be sorted
in order of increasing time.  In addition, we store the @newterm{current time}
(i.e., the time of the last action that was processed) alongside the
segments.  A newly constructed agenda has no time segments and has a current
time of 0:@footnote{The book's agenda is a headed list, like the tables in
@ref{3.3.3}, but since the list is headed by the time, it needs no additional
dummy header (such as the @code{*table*} symbol used with tables).  The edition
keeps the current time in a field of its own beside the segment chain, which
is why a segment scheduled at time 0 is ordinary, reachable work.}

@example typescript
interface Agenda @{
  currentTime: number;
  segments: MList<TimeSegment>;
@}

const makeAgenda = (): Agenda => (@{ currentTime: 0, segments: mnil @});
const currentAgendaTime = (agenda: Agenda): number => agenda.currentTime;
const setCurrentTime = (agenda: Agenda, time: number): void => @{
  agenda.currentTime = time;
@};
const segmentChain = (agenda: Agenda): MList<TimeSegment> => agenda.segments;
const setSegments = (agenda: Agenda, segments: MList<TimeSegment>): void => @{
  agenda.segments = segments;
@};
const firstSegment = (agenda: Agenda): TimeSegment => @{
  if (isMNil(agenda.segments)) throw new EmptyAgendaError();
  return agenda.segments.head;
@};
const restSegments = (agenda: Agenda): MList<TimeSegment> => @{
  if (isMNil(agenda.segments)) throw new EmptyAgendaError();
  return agenda.segments.tail;
@};
const firstSegmentCell = (agenda: Agenda): MCons<TimeSegment> => @{
  if (isMNil(agenda.segments)) throw new EmptyAgendaError();
  return agenda.segments;
@};
@end example

@noindent
An agenda is empty if it has no time segments:

@example typescript
const isAgendaEmpty = (agenda: Agenda): boolean => isMNil(agenda.segments);
@end example

@noindent
To add an action to an agenda, we first check if the agenda is empty.  If so,
we create a time segment for the action and install this in the agenda.
Otherwise, we scan the agenda, examining the time of each segment.  If we find
a segment for our appointed time, we add the action to the associated queue.
If we reach a time later than the one to which we are appointed, we insert a
new time segment into the agenda just before it.  If we reach the end of the
agenda, we must create a new time segment at the end.

@example typescript
const addToAgenda = (time: number, action: () => void, agenda: Agenda): void => @{
  const belongsBefore = (segments: MList<TimeSegment>): boolean =>
    isMNil(segments) || time < segments.head.time;
  const makeNewTimeSegment = (t: number, a: () => void): TimeSegment => (@{
    time: t,
    queue: insertQueue(makeQueue<() => void>(), a),
  @});
  const add = (segments: MList<TimeSegment>): void => @{
    if (isMNil(segments)) @{
      setSegments(agenda, mcons(makeNewTimeSegment(time, action), mnil));
      return;
    @}
    if (segments.head.time === time) @{
      insertQueue(segments.head.queue, action);
      return;
    @}
    if (belongsBefore(segments.tail)) @{
      setCdr(segments, mcons(makeNewTimeSegment(time, action), segments.tail));
      return;
    @}
    add(segments.tail);
  @};
  const segments = segmentChain(agenda);
  if (belongsBefore(segments)) @{
    setSegments(agenda, mcons(makeNewTimeSegment(time, action), segments));
    return;
  @}
  add(segments);
@};
@end example

@noindent
The module's own @code{addToAgenda} folds the @code{add} helper into a loop and
uses one predicate, @code{continuesPast}, for the scan, but it walks the same
chain and puts the action in the same place.

@noindent
The procedure that removes the first item from the agenda deletes the item at
the front of the queue in the first time segment.  If this deletion makes the
time segment empty, we remove it from the list of segments:@footnote{Observe
that the @code{if} expression in this procedure has no @code{⟨}@var{alternative}@code{⟩}
expression.  Such a ``one-armed @code{if} statement'' is used to decide whether
to do something, rather than to select between two expressions.  An @code{if}
expression returns an unspecified value if the predicate is false and there is
no @code{⟨}@var{alternative}@code{⟩}.}

@example typescript
const removeFirstAgendaItem = (agenda: Agenda): void => @{
  const cell = firstSegmentCell(agenda);
  const q = cell.head.queue;
  deleteQueue(q);
  if (isEmptyQueue(q)) @{
    setSegments(agenda, cell.tail);
  @}
@};
@end example

@noindent
The first agenda item is found at the head of the queue in the first time
segment.  Whenever we extract an item, we also update the current
time:@footnote{In this way, the current time will always be the time of the
action most recently processed.  Storing this time at the head of the agenda
ensures that it will still be available even if the associated time segment has
been deleted.}

@example typescript
const firstAgendaItem = (agenda: Agenda): (() => void) => @{
  const cell = firstSegmentCell(agenda);
  setCurrentTime(agenda, cell.head.time);
  return frontQueue(cell.head.queue);
@};
@end example

@noindent
Here @code{firstSegmentCell(agenda)} is the pair at the front of the segment
chain, and it throws the module's @code{EmptyAgendaError}, the book's
@i{Agenda is empty} error, when there is none.

@quotation
@strong{@anchor{Exercise 3.32}Exercise 3.32:} The procedures to be run during
each time segment of the agenda are kept in a queue.  Thus, the procedures for
each segment are called in the order in which they were added to the agenda
(first in, first out).  Explain why this order must be used.  In particular,
trace the behavior of an and-gate whose inputs change from @code{false},
@code{true} to @code{true}, @code{false} in the
same segment and say how the behavior would differ if we stored a segment's
procedures in an ordinary list, adding and removing procedures only at the
front (last in, first out).  Build @code{addToAgendaLifo} as a local variant
and compare its events with the module's FIFO @code{addToAgenda}.
@end quotation


@node	3.3.5, 3.4, 3.2, 3.3
@subsection Propagation of Constraints

Computer programs are traditionally organized as one-directional computations,
which perform operations on prespecified arguments to produce desired outputs.
On the other hand, we often model systems in terms of relations among
quantities.  For example, a mathematical model of a mechanical structure might
include the information that the deflection @math{d} of a metal rod is related to
the force @math{F} on the rod, the length @math{L} of the rod, the cross-sectional
area @math{A}, and the elastic modulus @math{E} via the equation
@displaymath
{dAE} \,=\, {FL.}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
dAE = FL
@end example

@end ifinfo
Such an equation is not one-directional.  Given any four of the quantities, we
can use it to compute the fifth.  Yet translating the equation into a
traditional computer language would force us to choose one of the quantities to
be computed in terms of the other four.  Thus, a procedure for computing the
area @math{A} could not be used to compute the deflection @math{d}, even though the
computations of @math{A} and @math{d} arise from the same
equation.@footnote{Constraint propagation first appeared in the incredibly
forward-looking @abbr{SKETCHPAD} system of Ivan @ref{Sutherland (1963)}.  A
beautiful constraint-propagation system based on the Smalltalk language was
developed by Alan @ref{Borning (1977)} at Xerox Palo Alto Research Center.  Sussman,
Stallman, and Steele applied constraint propagation to electrical circuit
analysis (@ref{Sussman and Stallman 1975}; @ref{Sussman and Steele 1980}). TK!Solver
(@ref{Konopasek and Jayaraman 1984}) is an extensive modeling environment based on
constraints.}

In this section, we sketch the design of a language that enables us to work in
terms of relations themselves.  The primitive elements of the language are
@newterm{primitive constraints}, which state that certain relations hold
between quantities.  For example, @code{adder(a, b, c)} specifies that the
quantities @math{a}, @math{b}, and @math{c} must be related by the equation
@math{{a + b = c}}, @code{multiplier(x, y, z)} expresses the constraint
@math{{xy = z}}, and @code{constant(3.14, x)} says that the value of @math{x}
must be 3.14.

Our language provides a means of combining primitive constraints in order to
express more complex relations.  We combine constraints by constructing
@newterm{constraint networks}, in which constraints are joined by
@newterm{connectors}.  A connector is an object that ``holds'' a value that may
participate in one or more constraints.  For example, we know that the
relationship between Fahrenheit and Celsius temperatures is
@displaymath
{9C} \,=\, {5(F - 32).}
@end displaymath
@ifinfo

@example
9C = 5(F - 32)
@end example

@end ifinfo
Such a constraint can be thought of as a network consisting of primitive adder,
multiplier, and constant constraints (@ref{Figure 3.28}).  In the figure, we
see on the left a multiplier box with three terminals, labeled @math{{m1}}, @math{{m2}},
and @math{p}.  These connect the multiplier to the rest of the network as follows:
The @math{{m1}} terminal is linked to a connector @math{C}, which will hold the Celsius
temperature.  The @math{{m2}} terminal is linked to a connector @math{w}, which is also
linked to a constant box that holds 9.  The @math{p} terminal, which the
multiplier box constrains to be the product of @math{{m1}} and @math{{m2}}, is linked to
the @math{p} terminal of another multiplier box, whose @math{{m2}} is connected to a
constant 5 and whose @math{{m1}} is connected to one of the terms in a sum.

@float
@anchor{Figure 3.28}
@ifinfo
@strong{Figure 3.28:} The relation @math{9C = 5(F - 32)} expressed as a constraint network.

@example
       +---------+     +---------+   v   +---------+
C -----+ m1      |  u  |      m1 +-------+ a1      |
       |    *  p +-----+ p  *    |       |    *  s +---- F
    +--+ m2      |     |      m2 +--+ +--+ a2      |
    |  +---------+     +---------+  | |  +---------+
  w |                              x| |y
    |    +-----+        +-----+     | |     +-----+
    +----+  9  |        |  5  +-----+ +-----+  32 |
         +-----+        +-----+             +-----+
@end example
@end ifinfo
@ifhtml
@image{../../text/original/figures/chap3/Fig3.28,,142mm,Figure 3.28: The relation 9C = 5(F - 32) expressed as a constraint network.,.std.svg}
@end ifhtml
@iftex
@image{../../text/original/figures/pdf/chap3/Fig3.28.std,,142mm,,.pdf}

@end iftex
@caption{@strong{Figure 3.28:} The relation @math{{9C = 5(F - 32)}} expressed as a constraint network.}
@end float

@noindent
Computation by such a network proceeds as follows: When a connector is given a
value (by the user or by a constraint box to which it is linked), it awakens
all of its associated constraints (except for the constraint that just awakened
it) to inform them that it has a value.  Each awakened constraint box then
polls its connectors to see if there is enough information to determine a value
for a connector.  If so, the box sets that connector, which then awakens all of
its associated constraints, and so on.  For instance, in conversion between
Celsius and Fahrenheit, @math{w}, @math{x}, and @math{y} are immediately set by the
constant boxes to 9, 5, and 32, respectively.  The connectors awaken the
multipliers and the adder, which determine that there is not enough information
to proceed.  If the user (or some other part of the network) sets @math{C} to a
value (say 25), the leftmost multiplier will be awakened, and it will set @math{u}
to @math{{25 \cdot 9 = 225}}.  Then @math{u} awakens the second multiplier, which sets @math{v} to
45, and @math{v} awakens the adder, which sets @math{f} to 77.

@subsubheading Using the constraint system

To use the constraint system to carry out the temperature computation outlined
above, we first create two connectors, @code{C} and @code{F}, by calling the
constructor @code{makeConnector}, and link @code{C} and @code{F} in an
appropriate network:

@example typescript
const C = makeConnector();
const F = makeConnector();
celsiusFahrenheitConverter(C, F);
@end example

@noindent
The procedure that creates the network is defined as follows:

@example typescript
const celsiusFahrenheitConverter = (c: Connector, f: Connector): void => @{
  const u = makeConnector();
  const v = makeConnector();
  const w = makeConnector();
  const x = makeConnector();
  const y = makeConnector();
  multiplier(c, w, u);
  multiplier(v, x, u);
  adder(v, y, f);
  constant(9, w);
  constant(5, x);
  constant(32, y);
@};
@end example

@noindent
This procedure creates the internal connectors @code{u}, @code{v}, @code{w},
@code{x}, and @code{y}, and links them as shown in @ref{Figure 3.28} using the
primitive constraint constructors @code{adder}, @code{multiplier}, and
@code{constant}.  Just as with the digital-circuit simulator of 
@ref{3.3.4}, expressing these combinations of primitive elements in terms of
procedures automatically provides our language with a means of abstraction for
compound objects.

To watch the network in action, we can place probes on the connectors @code{C}
and @code{F}, using a @code{connectorProbe} procedure similar to the
@code{probe} we used to
monitor wires in @ref{3.3.4}.  Placing a probe on a connector will
cause an event to be recorded whenever the connector is given a value:

@example typescript
const events: ConnectorProbeEvent[] = [];
connectorProbe("Celsius temp", C, events);
connectorProbe("Fahrenheit temp", F, events);
@end example

@noindent
Next we set the value of @code{C} to 25.  (The second argument to
@code{setValue} tells @code{C} that this directive comes from the
@code{"user"}.)

@example typescript
C.setValue(25, "user")
// events: @i{[@{ name: "Celsius temp", value: 25 @}, @{ name: "Fahrenheit temp", value: 77 @}]}
@end example

@noindent
The probe on @code{C} awakens and reports the value.  @code{C} also propagates
its value through the network as described above.  This sets @code{F} to 77,
which is reported by the probe on @code{F}.  The two readings arrive in the
order the book's @i{Probe:} lines print them.

Now we can try to set @code{F} to a new value, say 212:

@example typescript
F.setValue(212, "user")
// throws ContradictionError: @i{Contradiction: (77 212)}
@end example

@noindent
The connector complains that it has sensed a contradiction: Its value is 77,
and someone is trying to set it to 212.  If we really want to reuse the network
with new values, we can tell @code{C} to forget its old value:

@example typescript
C.forgetValue("user")
// events: @i{[@{ name: "Celsius temp", value: "?" @}, @{ name: "Fahrenheit temp", value: "?" @}]}
@end example

@noindent
@code{C} finds that the @code{user}, who set its value originally, is now
retracting that value, so @code{C} agrees to lose its value, as shown by the
probe, and informs the rest of the network of this fact.  This information
eventually propagates to @code{F}, which now finds that it has no reason for
continuing to believe that its own value is 77.  Thus, @code{F} also gives up
its value, as shown by the probe.

Now that @code{F} has no value, we are free to set it to 212:

@example typescript
F.setValue(212, "user")
// events: @i{[@{ name: "Fahrenheit temp", value: 212 @}, @{ name: "Celsius temp", value: 100 @}]}
@end example

@noindent
This new value, when propagated through the network, forces @code{C} to have a
value of 100, and this is registered by the probe on @code{C}.  Notice that the
very same network is being used to compute @code{C} given @code{F} and to
compute @code{F} given @code{C}.  This nondirectionality of computation is the
distinguishing feature of constraint-based systems.

@subsubheading Implementing the constraint system

The constraint system is implemented via procedural objects with local state,
in a manner very similar to the digital-circuit simulator of 
@ref{3.3.4}.  Although the primitive objects of the constraint system are
somewhat more complex, the overall system is simpler, since there is no concern
about agendas and logic delays.

The basic operations on connectors are the following:

@itemize @bullet

@item
@code{connector.hasValue()} tells whether the connector has a value.

@item
@code{connector.getValue()} returns the connector's current value.

@item
@code{connector.setValue(⟨@var{new-value}⟩, ⟨@var{informant}⟩)}
indicates that the informant is requesting the connector to set its value to
the new value.

@item
@code{connector.forgetValue(⟨@var{retractor}⟩)} tells the connector
that the retractor is requesting it to forget its value.

@item
@code{connector.connect(⟨@var{new-constraint}⟩)} tells the connector
to participate in the new constraint.

@end itemize

@noindent
The connectors communicate with the constraints by means of the procedures
@code{informAboutValue}, which tells the given constraint that the connector
has a value, and @code{informAboutNoValue}, which tells the constraint that
the connector has lost its value.  A constraint is exactly the book's dispatch
procedure: a function that answers one of the two messages.

@code{Adder} constructs an adder constraint among summand connectors @code{a1}
and @code{a2} and a @code{sum} connector.  An adder is implemented as a
procedure with local state (the @code{me} function below), and the module's
@code{adder} returns it:

@example typescript
const adder = (a1: Connector, a2: Connector, sum: Connector): Constraint => @{
  const processNewValue = (): void => @{
    if (a1.hasValue() && a2.hasValue()) @{
      sum.setValue(a1.getValue() + a2.getValue(), me);
    @} else if (a1.hasValue() && sum.hasValue()) @{
      a2.setValue(sum.getValue() - a1.getValue(), me);
    @} else if (a2.hasValue() && sum.hasValue()) @{
      a1.setValue(sum.getValue() - a2.getValue(), me);
    @}
  @};
  const processForgetValue = (): void => @{
    sum.forgetValue(me);
    a1.forgetValue(me);
    a2.forgetValue(me);
    processNewValue();
  @};
  const me: Constraint = (message): void => @{
    if (message === "informAboutValue") @{
      processNewValue();
    @} else @{
      processForgetValue();
    @}
  @};
  a1.connect(me);
  a2.connect(me);
  sum.connect(me);
  return me;
@};
@end example

@noindent
@code{Adder} connects the new adder to the designated connectors and returns it
as its value.  The function @code{me}, which represents the adder, is the
book's dispatch: it answers the two messages a constraint can be sent.  The
following ``syntax interfaces'' (see
@ref{Footnote 155} in @ref{3.3.4}) are used in conjunction with
the dispatch:

@example typescript
const informAboutValue = (constraint: Constraint): void =>
  constraint("informAboutValue");
const informAboutNoValue = (constraint: Constraint): void =>
  constraint("informAboutNoValue");
@end example

@noindent
The adder's local procedure @code{process-new-value} is called when the adder
is informed that one of its connectors has a value. The adder first checks to
see if both @code{a1} and @code{a2} have values. If so, it tells @code{sum} to
set its value to the sum of the two addends.  The @code{informant} argument to
@code{setValue} is @code{me}, which is the adder object itself.  If @code{a1}
and @code{a2} do not both have values, then the adder checks to see if perhaps
@code{a1} and @code{sum} have values.  If so, it sets @code{a2} to the
difference of these two.  Finally, if @code{a2} and @code{sum} have values,
this gives the adder enough information to set @code{a1}.  If the adder is told
that one of its connectors has lost a value, it requests that all of its
connectors now lose their values.  (Only those values that were set by this
adder are actually lost.)  Then it runs @code{process-new-value}.  The reason
for this last step is that one or more connectors may still have a value (that
is, a connector may have had a value that was not originally set by the adder),
and these values may need to be propagated back through the adder.

A multiplier is very similar to an adder. It will set its @code{product} to 0
if either of the factors is 0, even if the other factor is not known.

@example typescript
const multiplier = (
  m1: Connector,
  m2: Connector,
  product: Connector,
): Constraint => @{
  const processNewValue = (): void => @{
    if ((m1.hasValue() && m1.getValue() === 0) || (m2.hasValue() && m2.getValue() === 0)) @{
      product.setValue(0, me);
    @} else if (m1.hasValue() && m2.hasValue()) @{
      product.setValue(m1.getValue() * m2.getValue(), me);
    @} else if (product.hasValue() && m1.hasValue()) @{
      m2.setValue(product.getValue() / m1.getValue(), me);
    @} else if (product.hasValue() && m2.hasValue()) @{
      m1.setValue(product.getValue() / m2.getValue(), me);
    @}
  @};
  const processForgetValue = (): void => @{
    product.forgetValue(me);
    m1.forgetValue(me);
    m2.forgetValue(me);
    processNewValue();
  @};
  const me: Constraint = (message): void => @{
    if (message === "informAboutValue") @{
      processNewValue();
    @} else @{
      processForgetValue();
    @}
  @};
  m1.connect(me);
  m2.connect(me);
  product.connect(me);
  return me;
@};
@end example

@noindent
A @code{constant} constructor simply sets the value of the designated
connector.  Any @code{informAboutValue} or @code{informAboutNoValue} message
sent to the constant box will throw an error.

@example typescript
const constant = (value: number, connector: Connector): Constraint => @{
  const me: Constraint = (): void => @{
    throw new Error("Unknown request: CONSTANT");
  @};
  connector.connect(me);
  connector.setValue(value, me);
  return me;
@};
@end example

@noindent
Finally, a probe records a reading about the setting or unsetting of
the designated connector:

@example typescript
const connectorProbe = (
  name: string,
  connector: Connector,
  events: ConnectorProbeEvent[],
): Constraint => @{
  const me: Constraint = (message): void => @{
    if (message === "informAboutValue") @{
      events.push(@{ name, value: connector.getValue() @});
    @} else @{
      events.push(@{ name, value: "?" @});
    @}
  @};
  connector.connect(me);
  return me;
@};
@end example


@subsubheading Representing connectors

A connector is represented as a closure with local state: @code{value}, the
current value of the connector; @code{informant}, the object that set the
connector's value; and @code{constraints}, a list of the constraints in which
the connector participates.

@example typescript
const makeConnector = (): Connector => @{
  let value: number | undefined;
  let informant: Informant | undefined;
  let constraints: Constraint[] = [];
  const setMyValue = (newValue: number, setter: Informant): void => @{
    if (value === undefined) @{
      value = newValue;
      informant = setter;
      forEachExcept(setter, "informAboutValue", constraints);
    @} else if (value !== newValue) @{
      throw new ContradictionError(value, newValue);
    @}
  @};
  const forgetMyValue = (retractor: Informant): void => @{
    if (retractor === informant) @{
      informant = undefined;
      value = undefined;
      forEachExcept(retractor, "informAboutNoValue", constraints);
    @}
  @};
  const connect = (newConstraint: Constraint): void => @{
    if (!constraints.includes(newConstraint)) @{
      constraints = [newConstraint, ...constraints];
    @}
    if (value !== undefined) @{
      informAboutValue(newConstraint);
    @}
  @};
  return @{
    hasValue: () => value !== undefined,
    getValue: () => @{
      if (value === undefined) throw new Error("connector has no value");
      return value;
    @},
    setValue: setMyValue,
    forgetValue: forgetMyValue,
    connect,
  @};
@};
@end example

@noindent
The module keeps the five operations as the fields of a @code{Connector}
object; the book reaches the same locals through a @code{me} dispatch that
answers the messages @code{has-value?}, @code{value}, @code{set-value!},
@code{forget}, and @code{connect}.  Where the book starts @code{value} and
@code{informant} as @code{false} and reads the third state off the informant in
@code{has-value?}, @code{undefined} says it directly.

The connector's local procedure @code{setMyValue} is called when there is a
request to set the connector's value.  If the connector does not currently have
a value, it will set its value and remember as @code{informant} the constraint
that requested the value to be set.@footnote{The @code{setter} might not be a
constraint.  In our temperature example, we used the string @code{"user"} as
the @code{setter}.}  Then the connector will notify all of its participating
constraints except the constraint that requested the value to be set.  This is
accomplished using the following iterator, which sends a designated message
to all items in a list except a given one:

@example typescript
const forEachExcept = (
  exception: unknown,
  message: ConstraintMessage,
  list: Constraint[],
): void => @{
  for (const constraint of list) @{
    if (constraint !== exception) @{
      constraint(message);
    @}
  @}
@};
@end example

@noindent
If a connector is asked to forget its value, it runs the local procedure
@code{forgetMyValue}, which first checks to make sure that the request is
coming from the same object that set the value originally.  If so, the
connector informs its associated constraints about the loss of the value.

The local procedure @code{connect} adds the designated new constraint to the
list of constraints if it is not already in that list.  Then, if the connector
has a value, it informs the new constraint of this fact.

The connector's @code{setValue}, @code{forgetValue}, and @code{connect} fields
are those local procedures, and the object the constructor returns is what
represents the connector.  The following wrappers play the part of the book's
syntax interfaces for the dispatch:

@example typescript
const hasValue = (connector: Connector): boolean => connector.hasValue();
const getValue = (connector: Connector): number => connector.getValue();
const setValue = (
  connector: Connector,
  newValue: number,
  informant: Informant,
): void => connector.setValue(newValue, informant);
const forgetValue = (connector: Connector, retractor: Informant): void =>
  connector.forgetValue(retractor);
const connect = (connector: Connector, newConstraint: Constraint): void =>
  connector.connect(newConstraint);
@end example

